From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Tue, 9 Sep 2026 16:45:00 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: pace autosave by the size of the state

God of War stuttered - picture and sound together, a few times a minute:

  16:28:18  Wrote savestate of 134217984 bytes
  16:28:28  Wrote savestate of 134217984 bytes
  16:28:38  Wrote savestate of 134217984 bytes

128MB every ten seconds, ~13MB/s sustained, and the emulator stalls for each
one. Ten seconds is upstream's figure and it suits what it was written for: a
PS1 autosave here is 4.7MB and has never been noticeable. PPSSPP's are
twenty-eight times that.

Pacing on how long CreateAutosave() takes does not work - measured, and it
changed nothing. It returns once the write is queued and a job thread does the
writing, so the call looks cheap however big the state is:

  T:65191  RetroPlayer[SAVE]: Saved state to ...
  T:65514  Wrote savestate of 134217984 bytes      (0.6s later, another thread)

The size is what costs, so pace on that: stat the state and hold the write rate
to 2MB/s. A 4.7MB state stays on its ten seconds because it is already inside
that budget; a 128MB one moves to about a minute. No per-core table, and it
follows a core whose state size changes.

Autosave stays on - it was asked for, and turning it off for one system is a
worse answer than making it affordable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayerAutoSave.cpp b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayerAutoSave.cpp
index 86b8d98..79d92f5 100644
--- a/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayerAutoSave.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/RetroPlayer/RetroPlayerAutoSave.cpp
@@ -8,8 +8,11 @@
 
 #include "RetroPlayerAutoSave.h"
 
+#include <algorithm>
+
 #include "URL.h"
 #include "games/GameSettings.h"
+#include "filesystem/File.h"
 #include "utils/log.h"
 
 using namespace KODI;
@@ -19,6 +22,19 @@ using namespace std::chrono_literals;
 namespace
 {
 constexpr auto AUTOSAVE_DURATION_SECS = 10s; // Auto-save every 10 seconds
+
+// How fast we are willing to write autosaves, in bytes per second.
+//
+// Ten seconds suits the states this was written for: a PS1 autosave here is
+// 4.7MB, which is well under this and keeps the ten seconds it has always had.
+// A PPSSPP state is 134,217,984 bytes - 128MB every ten seconds, ~13MB/s
+// sustained, and the emulator stalls for each one: picture and sound together,
+// six times a minute.
+//
+// Pacing on how long CreateAutosave() takes does not work, because it returns
+// once the write is queued and a job thread does the writing. The size is what
+// costs, so pace on that.
+constexpr uint64_t AUTOSAVE_BYTES_PER_SECOND = 2 * 1024 * 1024;
 }
 
 CRetroPlayerAutoSave::CRetroPlayerAutoSave(IAutoSaveCallback& callback,
@@ -43,9 +59,12 @@ void CRetroPlayerAutoSave::Process()
 {
   CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "RetroPlayer[SAVE]: Autosave thread started");
 
+  auto interval =
+      std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(AUTOSAVE_DURATION_SECS);
+
   while (!m_bStop)
   {
-    CThread::Sleep(AUTOSAVE_DURATION_SECS);
+    CThread::Sleep(interval);
 
     if (m_bStop)
       break;
@@ -57,7 +76,26 @@ void CRetroPlayerAutoSave::Process()
     {
       std::string savePath = m_callback.CreateAutosave();
       if (!savePath.empty())
+      {
         CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "RetroPlayer[SAVE]: Saved state to {}", CURL::GetRedacted(savePath));
+
+        struct __stat64 statBuffer = {};
+        if (XFILE::CFile::Stat(savePath, &statBuffer) == 0 && statBuffer.st_size > 0)
+        {
+          const auto size = static_cast<uint64_t>(statBuffer.st_size);
+          const auto paced = std::chrono::seconds(size / AUTOSAVE_BYTES_PER_SECOND);
+          const auto next = std::max(
+              std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(AUTOSAVE_DURATION_SECS),
+              std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(paced));
+
+          if (next != interval)
+          {
+            CLog::Log(LOGDEBUG, "RetroPlayer[SAVE]: State is {} bytes, autosaving every {}s",
+                      size, next.count() / 1000);
+            interval = next;
+          }
+        }
+      }
     }
   }
 
